// PHASE 2 - PAGE 15: INNER CHILD - INTRO
// Shadow Work Philosopher: 200+ words on Inner Child + attachment theory
// Mirroring technique included

#somatic_page(
  title: "Phase 2: Inner Child – The Original Wound",
  body_text: [
    #align(center)[
      _"It is never too late to have a happy childhood."_
      
      — Tom Robbins
    ]
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    You may feel a sudden tenderness in your chest as you read these words. Perhaps a lump rises in your throat, or your eyes begin to sting. This is your body remembering what your mind has long since filed away. *The child is still here. They have been waiting.*
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    #rect(fill: rgb("#F5F5F0"), stroke: 0.5pt + gray, inset: 15pt, radius: 2pt)[
      The shadow often begins in childhood. When we were small, we learned which parts of ourselves were acceptable and which were not. The parts that were rejected did not disappear—they were simply buried. This is what Jung called the *formation of the shadow*: the gradual exile of everything that threatened our belonging.
      
      Attachment theory reveals that children are biologically wired to maintain connection with their caregivers at all costs. When a parent says "stop crying" or "don't be so sensitive," the child does not think, "My parent is wrong." The child thinks, "*I* am wrong." And so the sensitive part is banished, the crying part is silenced, the wild part is tamed—all in service of survival.
      
      Your Inner Child is the keeper of these original wounds. But they also hold the keys to your purest creativity, wonder, and spontaneous joy. Reparenting is not about erasing your past—it is about *finally* giving your younger self the validation, protection, and unconditional acceptance they never received. The body remembers what the mind has suppressed. In this phase, we return to the body to find the child who has been waiting.
    ]
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    *In this phase, you will:*
    - Meet your Inner Child through visualization and somatic awareness
    - Map where childhood silencing is physically stored in your body
    - Identify the messages you received about being "too much" or "not enough"
    - Begin the sacred process of reparenting yourself
  ],
  image_path: none,
  layout_style: "bottom"
)
